The ntile() function in dplyr 1.0.0 gives different results than in version 0.8.3.  Looking at the function code, it has changed a great deal.  I show both functions in my reprex.  ntile_083 scales us as the value of n increases, but ntile1_100 does not, its output is invariant to n (as long as n >= x).  This is a big change in the output.  Was this the intented result?

```r
ntile_083 <- function (x = row_number(), n) {
  len <- sum(!is.na(x))
  if (len == 0L) {
    rep(NA_integer_, length(x))
  } else {
    as.integer(floor(n * (row_number(x) - 1)/len + 1))
  }
}

ntile_100 <- function (x = row_number(), n) {
  if (!missing(x)) {
    x <- row_number(x)
  }
  len <- length(x) - sum(is.na(x))
  n <- as.integer(floor(n))
  if (len == 0L) {
    rep(NA_integer_, length(x))
  } else {
    n_larger <- as.integer(len%%n)
    n_smaller <- as.integer(n - n_larger)
    size <- len/n
    larger_size <- as.integer(ceiling(size))
    smaller_size <- as.integer(floor(size))
    larger_threshold <- larger_size * n_larger
    bins <- if_else(x <= larger_threshold, 
                    (x + (larger_size - 1L)) / larger_size,
                    (x + (-larger_threshold + smaller_size - 1L)) / smaller_size + n_larger)
    as.integer(floor(bins))
  }
}

x <- seq(.1,1,.1)
ntile_083(x, 10)
ntile_083(x, 100)
ntile_083(x, 1000)

ntile_100(x, 10)
ntile_100(x, 100)
ntile_100(x, 1000)
```

