When initially posted I contemplated on making a matrix dispatch into the same functionally as used for bind_all(). Currently though I do it with pure R code, as matrices are simpler structures that requires less checks as they are simple vectors. With that in mind i propose a construct such as this

``` r
rbindMat <- function(x, ..., fill = NA) {
    if (inherits(x, 'matrix')) x <- list(x, ...)
    if (length(x) == 1) return(x[[1]])
    cols <- unique(unlist(lapply(x, colnames)))
    rows <- seq(sum(sapply(x, nrow)))
    ans <- matrix(fill, ncol = length(cols), nrow = length(rows), 
                  dimnames = list(NULL, cols))
    currentEnd <- 1
    rowN <- FALSE
    for (i in 1:length(x)) {
        rowInd <- seq(currentEnd, length.out = nrow(x[[i]]))
        ans[rowInd, colnames(x[[i]])] <- x[[i]]
        if (!is.null(rownames(x[[i]]))) {
            if (!rowN) {
                rowN <- TRUE
                rownames(ans) <- 1:nrow(ans)
            }
            rownames(ans)[rowInd] <- rownames(x[[i]])
        }
        currentEnd <- currentEnd + nrow(x[[i]])
    }
    ans
}
```

It contains a very un-R loop but the chance of this being used with huge numbers of matrices, triggering a slowdown, is slim.

Most of the logic is actually concerned with handling row names. This could be alleviated by requiring all or no input to have row names.

How does this approach suit you? I think most time is spend allocating space for the final matrix, and this can just as well be done in R than C++...

